ps -- Command

Print process status
ps [-] [-acdDfgklmnrtwx] [-c namelist] [-k memory]

ps prints one line per process: the fields, and then the command name and
arguments as the process itself holds them.  With no options it prints the TTY
and PID fields of the processes attached to the caller's own terminal.

It reads the process table out of kernel memory, so it needs a namelist to find
the table in -- /coherent, the running kernel -- and the memory devices listed
below.  It is not setuid: those devices are readable by everybody here.

Fields
    TTY	The controlling terminal, THREE COLUMNS WIDE.  ps reads /dev at
	startup and abbreviates the name of the matching device to fit:
	`console' becomes `con', a numbered line ttyNN becomes its number,
	a pseudo-terminal ttypN becomes pN, and any other name keeps its
	first three characters.  A process with no controlling terminal
	prints a single dash.  A device with no name in /dev prints its
	major and minor numbers in decimal, separated by a colon and
	truncated to the three columns, which is what a terminal whose node
	was removed or never made looks like.

    PID	Process id, and what kill(1) takes.

    GROUP  Process group: the pid of the group leader, which for a login
	session is the shell login(1) started.  Printed by -g, with or
	without -l.

    PPID   Pid of the parent.

    UID	Login name of the process's real uid, from /etc/passwd, or the uid
	itself in decimal when no entry has it.

    K	Size of the process in kilobytes: its text, data and stack
	segments, and with -r the user and auxiliary segments too.  Blank
	when it comes to zero, as it does for a kernel process, or a dash
	under -f.  A process whose segment table cannot be read prints ?K.

    F	Process flag bits, octal:

	    00001  in core
	    00002  locked in core
	    00004  swap I/O in progress
	    00010  swapped out
	    00020  stopped, not yet waited for
	    00040  stopped and waited on
	    00100  being traced
	    00200  kernel process
	    00400  auxiliary segments in memory
	    01000  dispatch at the earliest convenience
	    02000  command mode forbids a dump
	    04000  wakeup requested; only a kernel built with QWAKEUP
		   ever sets it

    S	State: R ready to run, S asleep, T stopped while asleep, W waiting
	for a child, Z a zombie whose parent has not waited, ? none of
	these.

    EVENT  What a sleeping process is waiting for: the address it will be
	woken on, eight hexadecimal digits, printed only for state S.
	It is the whole 32-bit address, segment and offset -- processes
	sleeping on the same channel show the same value and that is the
	only reading it supports.  There are no symbolic event names on
	this system.  Blank for any other state, or a dash under -f.

    CVAL SVAL IVAL RVAL
	Scheduling values; larger is better.  Printed by -m.

    UTIME STIME
	Time in the program and time in the system, as minutes:seconds, or
	as minutes alone once it passes 99 minutes.  Blank when the time
	rounds to zero, or a dash under -f.  Printed by -t.

Options
An option letter this ps does not know prints the usage line above and exits,
and so does a -c or -k with nothing after it.  A leading `-' is optional and
letters may be bundled.

    -a	Every process with a controlling terminal, not just the caller's.
    -c namelist
	Read the kernel namelist from this file instead of /coherent.  A
	file with no procq_ symbol in it is refused -- `Bad namelist
	file' -- but one that has the symbol and is not the kernel now
	running is not detected, and the listing is then nonsense.
    -d	Include kernel processes, which are otherwise never listed
	whatever -a and -x say.  Their command column shows the name the
	kernel gave them, in angle brackets.
    -D	Print the address of each process's table entry, eight
	hexadecimal digits, ahead of the TTY column.  ps's own usage line
	does not list it.
    -f	Put a dash in the K, EVENT, UTIME and STIME fields where they
	would be blank, so that awk(1) and sort(1) see a column there.
    -g	Print the GROUP field.
    -k memory
	Read process memory from this file instead of /dev/mem.
    -l	Long format: the PPID, UID, K, F, S and EVENT fields as well.
    -m	Print the scheduling fields.
    -n	No header line.
    -r	Count the user and auxiliary segments in K as well, which is
	every segment the process has.
    -t	Print the UTIME and STIME fields.
    -w	Wide output: 132 columns for the command and its arguments instead
	of 80.
    -x	Include processes with no controlling terminal.

Files
/coherent -- the running kernel, read for the namelist
/dev/kmem -- kernel memory, where the process table is read from
/dev/mem -- process memory, for the command name and arguments
/dev/swap -- opened when it exists, for a swapped-out process
/dev -- read at startup for the terminal names in the TTY column
/etc/passwd -- for the UID field

/dev/kmem, /dev/mem and /dev/swap are mode 600 and owned by root, so ps is
set-uid root.  It opens the three of them and then drops back to the real user,
so the listing itself is unprivileged.  -c and -k name files of the caller's
own, and ps drops the privilege before opening anything when either is given:
`ps -k file' can therefore read only what the user running it could read.

